8250: CE5300 GEN3 UARTs are XScale — force the type so UUE is set

The three SoC UARTs on the Intel CE5300 (EA family) are an XScale/PXA block --
Control4's kernel renames PORT_XSCALE's uart_config to "GEN3_serial" -- and the
IO microcontroller (relays, contacts, IR, combo-serial) hangs off ttyS1 at legacy
I/O 0x2F8. A GEN3/XScale port is meant to run with UART_CAP_UUE (the UART Unit
Enable bit, IER b6) and UART_CAP_RTOIE, matching stock's "GEN3_serial" config and
its put_poll_char, which forces UUE on. Getting the type right is a prerequisite:
it is also what patch 0010's poll-TX fix keys on (port->type == PORT_XSCALE). The
actual TX/RX bring-up -- why tx counted while rx stayed 0 -- is the polled-vs-IRQ
story in patches 0002 and 0010; this patch just makes the ports the XScale part
they physically are.

autoconfig_16550a can find an XScale part by toggling IER b6, but two things stop
it here: (1) this kernel disables CONFIG_SERIAL_8250_16550A_VARIANTS, so the
function sets PORT_16550A and returns immediately, never reaching the XScale probe;
and (2) even reached, that probe is unreliable this early -- the block is not
clocked yet so the UUE bit does not latch (it IS writable once the port is opened;
measured on hardware). So force the type for the three fixed SoC I/O bases up front,
before the early return, and leave UUE enabled. This keeps them as normal I/O ISA
ports -- declaring PORT_XSCALE + UPF_FIXED_TYPE in SERIAL_PORT_DFNS instead makes
serial8250_isa_init_ports drop them and the 2e66 PCI UARTs steal the ttyS numbers.
The 8051 power PIC at 0x3E8 is a genuine 8250 and is deliberately not matched,
matching stock's /proc/tty/driver/serial. Pairs with patch 0002 (clock + IRQ 38).

Scope: EA family only (board/ea-common/patches).

Author: Joseph Telaak <joseph@telaak.dev>
---
--- a/drivers/tty/serial/8250/8250_port.c
+++ b/drivers/tty/serial/8250/8250_port.c
@@ -915,6 +915,26 @@
 
 	up->port.type = PORT_16550A;
 	up->capabilities |= UART_CAP_FIFO;
+
+	/*
+	 * Intel CE5300 (EA family): three legacy I/O UARTs are the SoC's own
+	 * XScale/PXA block ("GEN3_serial" in Control4's kernel); the IO-MCU is on
+	 * ttyS1 (0x2F8). They MUST run with UART_CAP_UUE -- the UART Unit Enable bit
+	 * (IER b6) that gates whether the block drives its pins -- plus UART_CAP_RTOIE
+	 * so a short reply's rx-timeout interrupt fires. The generic UUE probe below
+	 * would find them, but it sits after the early return taken when
+	 * CONFIG_SERIAL_8250_16550A_VARIANTS is off, and it is unreliable this early
+	 * anyway (the unit is not clocked, so UUE does not latch). Force the type by
+	 * the fixed SoC I/O bases, here, before that return. The 8051 PIC at 0x3E8 is
+	 * a genuine 8250 and is deliberately not matched.
+	 */
+	if (up->port.iobase == 0x3F8 || up->port.iobase == 0x2F8 ||
+	    up->port.iobase == 0x2E8) {
+		up->port.type = PORT_XSCALE;
+		up->capabilities |= UART_CAP_UUE | UART_CAP_RTOIE;
+		serial_out(up, UART_IER, UART_IER_UUE);
+		return;
+	}
 
 	if (!IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_SERIAL_8250_16550A_VARIANTS) &&
 	    !(up->port.flags & UPF_FULL_PROBE))
